---
import { Code } from "astro:components";
import codeTheme from "../styles/shiki-pitch.json";
import ProjectPage from "../layouts/ProjectPage.astro";
import { href, link, menu } from "../consts";

const releases = "https://github.com/Super-Yojan/terra-image-gen/releases";
const osList = "https://github.com/Super-Yojan/terra-image-gen/releases/latest/download/terra-os-list.json";
const macos = `open -a 'Raspberry Pi Imager' --args --repo ${osList}`;
---

<ProjectPage
    title="Terra image"
    description="The Terra SD card image: 64-bit Raspberry Pi OS Bookworm for the Pi 4 and Pi 5 with the Fusion HAT driver and terra-rover installed."
    lede="One SD card image for every Terra rover. Flash it with Raspberry Pi Imager and the Pi boots ready to drive."
    figureLabel="Fig. 01 — Terra in Raspberry Pi Imager"
    figureAlt="The Terra icon: a six-wheeled rover on a forest road, as it appears in the Imager OS list."
    poster={href("/media/terra-icon.png")}
    posterWidth={480}
    posterHeight={480}
    figureNote="Terra (64-bit) in the Imager list"
    primary={{ href: "#quick-start", label: "Get Started" }}
    secondary={{ href: releases, label: "Download" }}
    aboutTitle="Raspberry Pi OS, ready for the rover."
    sectionId="quick-start"
    sectionTitle="Quick start."
    sectionLede="From a blank card to SSH on the Pi. The guides below cover each step in full."
    links={menu.map((page) => ({ href: link(page.path), label: page.label }))}
>
    <Fragment slot="prose">
        <p>
            The Terra image is 64-bit Raspberry Pi OS Bookworm with the Fusion
            HAT kernel driver and the <code>terra-rover</code> service already
            installed. Flash the same image to any number of cards: each card
            gets its own hostname, machine-id and SSH host keys on first boot.
        </p>
        <p>
            The image contains no password, Wi-Fi password, SSH key or paired
            phone. Raspberry Pi Imager adds the login, Wi-Fi and SSH while it
            writes the card, and you pair TerraPhone after boot.
        </p>
        <p>
            It is built with rpi-image-gen from <code>examples/terra</code>.
            The <a href={link("/hardware-checklist")}>hardware checklist</a>
            lists what has not yet been checked on a real Pi.
        </p>
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="facts">
        <div>
            <dt class="t-label">Boards</dt>
            <dd>Raspberry Pi 4 and Pi 5, 64-bit</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
            <dt class="t-label">OS</dt>
            <dd>Raspberry Pi OS Bookworm, arm64</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
            <dt class="t-label">Rover service</dt>
            <dd><code>terra-rover</code> <code>0.3.0</code></dd>
        </div>
        <div>
            <dt class="t-label">Card</dt>
            <dd>microSD, 8 GB or more</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
            <dt class="t-label">USB SSH</dt>
            <dd><code>192.168.77.1</code> (Pi 4)</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
            <dt class="t-label">Hotspot</dt>
            <dd><code>Terra-xxxxxx</code>, <code>192.168.78.1</code></dd>
        </div>
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="steps">
        <li>
            <p class="t-label">01</p>
            <div class="prose">
                <h3>Open Imager on the Terra list</h3>
                <p>
                    Install <a href="https://www.raspberrypi.com/software/">Raspberry Pi Imager</a>
                    1.8 or newer, quit it, then open it on the Terra OS list. On macOS:
                </p>
                <Code code={macos} lang="sh" theme={codeTheme} wrap={false} />
                <p>
                    <a href={link("/flash")}>Flash a card</a> has the Linux and Windows commands.
                </p>
            </div>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p class="t-label">02</p>
            <div class="prose">
                <h3>Choose and customise</h3>
                <p>
                    Pick your Pi, <strong>Terra (64-bit)</strong> and the card.
                    In <strong>Edit settings</strong>, set a username, password,
                    Wi-Fi and SSH. Then write.
                </p>
            </div>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p class="t-label">03</p>
            <div class="prose">
                <h3>Boot and log in</h3>
                <p>
                    The first start applies your settings and reboots once.
                    Give it about two minutes, then
                    <code>ssh USERNAME@HOSTNAME.local</code>.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <a href={link("/build")}>Build the image</a> covers USB, the
                    hotspot and Wi-Fi from the boot partition.
                </p>
            </div>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p class="t-label">04</p>
            <div class="prose">
                <h3>Pair TerraPhone</h3>
                <p>
                    Hold the Fusion HAT <strong>USR</strong> button for three
                    seconds and accept <strong>Connect</strong> in TerraPhone.
                    The <a href="https://super-yojan.dev/Terra/hardware/FLASH_SD/">Terra
                    guide</a> has screenshots of every step.
                </p>
            </div>
        </li>
    </Fragment>
</ProjectPage>
